  "claim": "The well preserved helmet discovered in 1939 with the helmet similarly designed as the  Pioneer Helmet,  is widely believed to have been the helmet of Gadkaris of East Anglia.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Coppergate Helmet. Sentence: It is remarkably well preserved and, together with the Benty Grange, Pioneer, Shorwell, Sutton Hoo, and Staffordshire helmets, is one of only six Anglo-Saxon helmets discovered to date.\nPage: Sutton Hoo helmet. Sentence: It was buried around 625 and is widely believed to have been the helmet of King Rædwald of East Anglia, and its elaborate decoration may have given it a secondary function akin to a crown.\nPage: Pioneer Helmet. Sentence: This helmet is very similar in its basic design to the Coppergate Helmet, although it is much larger, and was likely to have had two cheek plates (of which only one remained) and a nasal (which was bent inwards at the time of deposition to render the piece unwearable).",
