      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "9d6a9243-0f88-50b6-9c9a-ec8b459cb8cf",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Coppergate Helmet, Sutton Hoo helmet, Pioneer Helmet\n- Topics: Anglo-Saxon helmets, helmet design, burial context\n\nFacts:\n- The Sutton Hoo helmet was buried around 625.\n- The Sutton Hoo helmet is widely believed to have been the helmet of King Rædwald of East Anglia.\n- The Sutton Hoo helmet had elaborate decoration that may have given it a secondary function similar to a crown.\n- The Coppergate Helmet is remarkably well preserved.\n- The Coppergate Helmet, along with the Benty Grange, Pioneer, Shorwell, Sutton Hoo, and Staffordshire helmets, is one of only six Anglo-Saxon helmets discovered to date.\n- The Pioneer Helmet is very similar to the Coppergate Helmet in its basic design.\n- The Pioneer Helmet is much larger than the Coppergate Helmet.\n- The Pioneer Helmet likely originally had two cheek plates, although only one remained.\n- The Pioneer Helmet likely had a nasal that was bent inward at the time of deposition to render the helmet unwearable.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f13820c9-d87c-54ec-b674-f80afad24064",
        "text": "Page: Coppergate Helmet. Sentence: It is remarkably well preserved and, together with the Benty Grange, Pioneer, Shorwell, Sutton Hoo, and Staffordshire helmets, is one of only six Anglo-Saxon helmets discovered to date.\nPage: Sutton Hoo helmet. Sentence: It was buried around 625 and is widely believed to have been the helmet of King Rædwald of East Anglia, and its elaborate decoration may have given it a secondary function akin to a crown.\nPage: Pioneer Helmet. Sentence: This helmet is very similar in its basic design to the Coppergate Helmet, although it is much larger, and was likely to have had two cheek plates (of which only one remained) and a nasal (which was bent inwards at the time of deposition to render the piece unwearable).",
